**Lemma 3.1 (spectral failure separation at height equality).** Define

$$
\lambda_\epsilon=\frac{-d_f+\epsilon L_f}{2},\qquad
f_\epsilon=\frac{(z_f,\lambda_\epsilon+d_f)^T}
 {\sqrt{z_f^2+(\lambda_\epsilon+d_f)^2}},\qquad
b_\epsilon=\langle f_\epsilon|M^{1/2},\quad
B_\epsilon=b_\epsilon^\dagger b_\epsilon.
\tag{TA.15}
$$

Extend each row by zero on input coordinate $0$. Then
$\lambda_+>0>\lambda_-$, $B_++B_-=M$ and
$\operatorname{tr}(B_\epsilon\Delta)=\lambda_\epsilon$.
For every height optimizer, each nonzero supported later-failure effect
at an actual label is a positive multiple of exactly one $B_\epsilon$.
Each spectral sign has total multiple one. At a label also carrying live
mass, its live height difference has the same sign as its failure
difference, or is zero. One label cannot contain positive masses of
both spectral signs.

Proof. The two vectors in TA.15 are orthonormal eigenvectors of $\Xi$;
their denominators are positive because $z_f\ne0$. For arbitrary
supported later-failure effects $B_\gamma\succeq0$ summing to $M$,
write $B_\gamma=M^{1/2}E_\gamma M^{1/2}$ on $\operatorname{ran}M$,
where $E_\gamma\succeq0$ and $\sum E_\gamma=I_2$. Put
$a_\pm=\langle f_\pm,E_\gamma f_\pm\rangle\geq0$. Then

$$
|\operatorname{tr}(E_\gamma\Xi)|
 =|\lambda_+a_++\lambda_-a_-|
 \leq\lambda_+a_+-\lambda_-a_-
 =\operatorname{tr}(E_\gamma|\Xi|).
\tag{TA.16}
$$

Equality holds precisely when at least one $a_\pm$ is zero. Positivity
makes a matrix annihilate a vector with zero quadratic form. A nonzero
equality summand therefore equals a positive multiple of one eigenline
projector, proving the asserted form of $B_\gamma$.

For weak instruments the credited reduction gives

$$
2T_{\rm actual}\leq
 (1-\delta)\Delta A+L_f+\sum_\gamma|f_\gamma^TGf_\gamma|
 \leq2\mathcal T(s).
\tag{TA.17}
$$

The one-ray lemma supplies $f_\gamma$ for each actual low live-bearing
label; their grouped maximizing-report/sign tuple is feasible because
the original contrast is at most $s_0\sum|f_{\gamma0}f_{\gamma1}|$.
The first inequality separates live and failure height differences by
the triangle inequality and uses TA.16. At height equality both gaps
are zero. Thus every spectral slack and every live/failure triangle
slack is zero, giving all the claims. In the strong regime JR's sharp
height formula is exactly the analogous sum over its fixed low/high
ray differences and $L_f$. The identical nonnegative-slack argument
applies. Sums become integrals under the stipulated hypotheses, and
zero nonnegative integrated slack vanishes almost everywhere. $\square$

